    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d498">

                <pb facs="0755" n="741" xml:id="pg741"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.20211/4–1046: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Smith</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-04-10T13:00:00+03:00">April 10, 1946—1
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received April 10—8:09 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1119. BBC this morning reported that Dept has had exchange of correspondence with
                    Soviet Govt concerning Soviet naval officer who was recently arrested by US
                    authorities for espionage. I wish to request that I be completely informed
                    currently of any matter of this sort having to do with activities and treatment
                    of Soviet official personnel in US.<anchor n="81" xml:id="d498-f1"/>
                    Dept will understand that in instances of this
                    sort Soviet retaliation is always a possibility and we here should be aware in
                    particular of details of any case which may bring counter action against
                    American personnel in USSR.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Smith</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d498-f1" n="81" xml:id="d498-f1-content">
                <p>Telegram 665, April 10, 1946, to Moscow, contained a summary of the developments
                    in this case between April 6 and 9. (861.20211/4–1046)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
